(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Memory Interfacing and Bus Multiplexing of the 8051 Microcontroller using ALE and 74HC573 latch.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   8051 Memory Interfacing and Address/Data Demultiplexing:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Pin-Limitation Problem:) Tj
T*
(   - To access 64 KB of external memory, 16 address lines \(A0-A15\) and 8 data lines \(D0-D7\) are) Tj
T*
(   required \(24 physical lines\).) Tj
T*
(   - In a standard 40-pin DIP package, dedicating 24 pins exclusively to memory buses would leave) Tj
T*
(   insufficient pins for I/O ports.) Tj
T*
(   2. Multiplexed Bus Solution:) Tj
T*
(   - Port 0 is designed as a time-multiplexed Address/Data bus: it carries low-order address lines) Tj
T*
(   A0-A7 during the first phase of a machine cycle, and bidirectional data lines D0-D7 during the) Tj
T*
(   second phase.) Tj
T*
(   - Port 2 provides high-order address lines A8-A15 continuously.) Tj
T*
(   3. Demultiplexing using Address Latch Enable \(ALE\) and 74HC573 Latch:) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: At the start of every external memory access, 8051 outputs low-order address A0-A7 on) Tj
T*
(   Port 0 and asserts the ALE pin HIGH \(+5 V\).) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: The HIGH ALE pulse enables transparent octal latch 74HC573 \(or 74LS373\), passing) Tj
T*
(   A0-A7 through to its output pins.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: ALE transitions from HIGH to LOW. The falling edge freezes \(latches\) the address bits) Tj
T*
(   A0-A7 inside the latch, holding them stable on the external address bus for the remainder of) Tj
T*
(   the memory cycle.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 4: Port 0 pins are released into high-impedance state, ready to transmit or receive data) Tj
T*
(   bits D0-D7.) Tj
T*
(   4. Control Signals:) Tj
T*
(   - PSEN' \(Program Store Enable\): Active-low strobe to read external Program ROM.) Tj
T*
(   - RD' \(Port 3.7\) and WR' \(Port 3.6\): Active-low strobes to read from and write to external Data) Tj
T*
(   RAM.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the 8051 Interrupt Structure, detailing interrupt sources, vector table, priority registers, and ISR execution sequence.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   8051 Microcontroller Interrupt Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   1. Interrupt Sources and Fixed Hardware Vector Addresses:) Tj
T*
(   - Reset \(Power-on/Hardware Reset\): Vector 0000H.) Tj
T*
(   - External Interrupt 0 \(INT0', Pin P3.2\): Vector 0003H \(Priority 1\).) Tj
T*
(   - Timer 0 Overflow Interrupt \(TF0\): Vector 000BH \(Priority 2\).) Tj
T*
(   - External Interrupt 1 \(INT1', Pin P3.3\): Vector 0013H \(Priority 3\).) Tj
T*
(   - Timer 1 Overflow Interrupt \(TF1\): Vector 001BH \(Priority 4\).) Tj
T*
(   - Serial Communication Interrupt \(RI / TI\): Vector 0023H \(Priority 5\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Interrupt Enable Register \(IE, Address A8H\):) Tj
T*
(   - EA \(IE.7\): Global Interrupt Enable bit. Must be set to 1 for any interrupt to fire.) Tj
T*
(   - Individual enable bits: ET2, ES \(Serial\), ET1 \(Timer 1\), EX1 \(External 1\), ET0 \(Timer 0\), EX0) Tj
T*
(   \(External 0\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Interrupt Priority Register \(IP, Address B8H\):) Tj
T*
(   - Assigns either High Priority or Low Priority to each interrupt source.) Tj
T*
(   - A high-priority interrupt can preempt \(nest inside\) an actively running low-priority ISR; a) Tj
T*
(   low-priority interrupt cannot interrupt another low-priority ISR.) Tj
T*
(   4. Hardware Execution Sequence upon Interrupt Trigger:) Tj
T*
ET

