(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Optical Rotary Encoders: Incremental versus Absolute Encoders, and Gray code disks.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Optical Rotary Encoders for Motion Control:) Tj
T*
(   1. Incremental Rotary Encoders:) Tj
T*
(   - Construction: Transparent glass or plastic disc patterned with alternating opaque and) Tj
T*
(   transparent radial slits, rotating between LED light sources and photodetectors.) Tj
T*
(   - Quadrature Channels \(A and B\): Two output pulse tracks electrically displaced by 90 degrees) Tj
T*
(   in space.) Tj
T*
(   * Pulse Count: Measures incremental angular displacement and rotational speed: omega =) Tj
T*
(   \(Delta-theta\) / dt.) Tj
T*
(   * Phase Direction: If Channel A leads Channel B, shaft rotates clockwise; if B leads A,) Tj
T*
(   rotation is counterclockwise.) Tj
T*
(   - Index \(Z\) Channel: A single pulse per full revolution providing an absolute reference) Tj
T*
(   zero/home mark.) Tj
T*
(   - Limitation: Volatile; total position information is lost upon power failure.) Tj
T*
(   2. Absolute Rotary Encoders:) Tj
T*
(   - Construction: Disc patterned with multiple concentric tracks, each read by a dedicated radial) Tj
T*
(   photodetector array, generating a unique multi-bit digital word for every discrete angular) Tj
T*
(   shaft position.) Tj
T*
(   - Retains absolute angular position even if moved while powered OFF.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Necessity of Gray Code Disks:) Tj
T*
(   - In standard binary code, multiple bit transitions occur simultaneously at certain boundaries) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., 0111 to 1000 toggles all 4 bits\). Due to physical manufacturing misalignments, sensors) Tj
T*
(   transition asynchronously, generating catastrophic momentary false position codes.) Tj
T*
(   - Gray Code features the Unit-Distance Property: exactly ONE bit changes between any two) Tj
T*
(   consecutive angular positions, completely eliminating read errors.) Tj
T*
