ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Real-Time Operating Systems \(RTOS\) in Embedded Systems: Preemptive scheduling, Priority Inversion, and Priority Inheritance.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Real-Time Operating Systems \(RTOS\) in Embedded Systems:) Tj
T*
(   1. Real-Time Determinism:) Tj
T*
(   - A real-time operating system guarantees that critical tasks complete execution within rigid,) Tj
T*
(   deterministic time boundaries \(deadlines\), where system correctness depends on timing as well) Tj
T*
(   as logical computation.) Tj
T*
(   2. Preemptive Priority-Based Scheduling:) Tj
T*
(   - Tasks are assigned static or dynamic priorities.) Tj
T*
(   - The RTOS scheduler guarantees that the processor always executes the highest-priority task) Tj
T*
(   that is ready to run.) Tj
T*
(   - If a higher-priority task is unblocked by an interrupt or timer, the scheduler immediately) Tj
T*
(   preempts the running lower-priority task, saving its context and executing the high-priority) Tj
T*
(   task.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Priority Inversion Hazard:) Tj
T*
(   - Occurs when a high-priority task \(H\) requires a shared mutual exclusion resource \(Mutex\)) Tj
T*
(   currently locked by a low-priority task \(L\). Task H enters blocked state.) Tj
T*
(   - A medium-priority task \(M\) that does not require the mutex preempts task L due to higher) Tj
T*
(   priority.) Tj
T*
(   - Consequently, task L cannot finish and release the mutex, indirectly blocking task H) Tj
T*
(   indefinitely! \(This famous bug crippled the Mars Pathfinder spacecraft in 1997\).) Tj
T*
(   4. Solution: Priority Inheritance Protocol:) Tj
T*
(   - When high-priority task H blocks on a mutex held by low-priority task L, task L temporarily) Tj
T*
(   inherits the priority of task H.) Tj
T*
(   - Medium-priority task M can no longer preempt task L.) Tj
T*
ET

