BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Distributed Control Systems \(DCS\) versus Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition \(SCADA\) in industrial plants.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   DCS versus SCADA in Industrial Process Automation:) Tj
T*
(   1. Distributed Control System \(DCS\):) Tj
T*
(   - Architecture: Process-oriented system designed for large, geographically localized continuous) Tj
T*
(   industrial plants \(e.g., oil refineries, chemical plants, thermal power stations\).) Tj
T*
(   - Control Philosophy: High reliability through distributed autonomous local controllers.) Tj
T*
(   Control loops are closed locally inside individual field controllers with cycle times < 50 ms.) Tj
T*
(   - System Integration: Tightly integrated proprietary hardware and software ecosystem from a) Tj
T*
(   single vendor with unified database, automated tag management, and integrated operator) Tj
T*
(   consoles. High availability via redundant power, CPUs, and networks.) Tj
T*
(   2. Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition \(SCADA\):) Tj
T*
(   - Architecture: Data-gathering-oriented system designed for large, geographically dispersed) Tj
T*
(   assets spanning hundreds of kilometers \(e.g., municipal water pipelines, oil/gas cross-country) Tj
T*
(   pipelines, electrical transmission grids\).) Tj
T*
(   - Communication: Relies on long-distance, potentially intermittent communication channels) Tj
T*
(   \(cellular telemetry, radio links, satellite\).) Tj
T*
(   - Control Philosophy: Field control is executed by autonomous Remote Terminal Units \(RTUs\) and) Tj
T*
(   PLCs; the central SCADA server focuses on supervisory monitoring, historical archiving, trend) Tj
T*
(   logging, and alarming.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 25:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Safety Instrumented Systems \(SIS\), Emergency Shutdown \(ESD\), and Safety Integrity Levels \(SIL\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Safety Instrumented Systems \(SIS\) and Functional Safety:) Tj
T*
(   1. Layer of Protection Analysis \(LOPA\):) Tj
T*
(   - Industrial plants utilize multiple defensive rings: Basic Process Control System \(BPCS\) ->) Tj
T*
(   Critical Alarms -> Safety Instrumented System \(SIS\) -> Physical Relief Devices \(rupture discs\)) Tj
T*
(   -> Emergency Dike Containment.) Tj
T*
(   - An SIS is an independent, dedicated safety architecture engineered to take a plant process to) Tj
T*
(   a safe state when predetermined limits are violated.) Tj
T*
(   2. Core SIS Components:) Tj
T*
(   - Safety Sensors: Redundant transmitters \(e.g., 2oo3 - 2-out-of-3 voting pressure sensors\).) Tj
T*
(   - Safety Logic Solver: TMR \(Triple Modular Redundant\) fail-safe safety PLCs certified to IEC) Tj
T*
(   61508.) Tj
T*
(   - Final Control Elements: Emergency Shutdown Valves \(ESDVs\) with fail-safe spring return) Tj
T*
(   actuators.) Tj
T*
(   3. Safety Integrity Levels \(SIL 1 to SIL 4\):) Tj
T*
(   - Defined under IEC 61508 / IEC 61511 according to the required Probability of Failure on) Tj
T*
(   Demand \(PFD\):) Tj
T*
(   * SIL 1: PFD = 10^-1 to 10^-2 \(Risk reduction factor 10 to 100\).) Tj
T*
(   * SIL 2: PFD = 10^-2 to 10^-3 \(Risk reduction factor 100 to 1,000\).) Tj
T*
(   * SIL 3: PFD = 10^-3 to 10^-4 \(Risk reduction factor 1,000 to 10,000\).) Tj
T*
(   * SIL 4: PFD = 10^-4 to 10^-5 \(Nuclear power and catastrophic risk mitigation\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
ET

