BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Watchdog Timer \(WDT\) and brown-out reset circuitry in mission-critical embedded systems.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Watchdog Timers and Brown-Out Protection in Embedded Systems:) Tj
T*
(   1. Watchdog Timer \(WDT\) Operational Principle:) Tj
T*
(   - An independent free-running hardware timer clocked by a dedicated on-chip low-frequency RC) Tj
T*
(   oscillator, separate from the main system crystal.) Tj
T*
(   - During normal operation, the main application firmware must periodically reset \('kick' or) Tj
T*
(   'service'\) the WDT register before its counter overflows.) Tj
T*
(   - If the firmware becomes trapped in an infinite deadlock loop, corrupted by an EMI voltage) Tj
T*
(   spike, or crashes due to stack overflow, the firmware fails to service the WDT.) Tj
T*
(   - The WDT counter reaches overflow and issues a forced hardware Reset, rebooting the) Tj
T*
(   microcontroller into a known safe state.) Tj
T*
(   2. Windowed Watchdog Timer \(WWDT\):) Tj
T*
(   - Requires the firmware to service the timer within a specific time window \(neither too late) Tj
T*
(   NOR too early\).) Tj
T*
(   - Catches runaway execution where corrupted code loops rapidly through service routines.) Tj
T*
(   3. Brown-Out Reset \(BOR\) Circuit:) Tj
T*
(   - Constantly monitors supply voltage Vdd against an internal precision bandgap reference.) Tj
T*
(   - If Vdd sags below critical operational threshold \(e.g., 2.7 V\), the CPU can misread) Tj
T*
(   instructions, corrupting Flash or EEPROM memory.) Tj
T*
(   - BOR immediately asserts a solid hardware reset, holding CPU halted until supply voltage) Tj
T*
(   recovers cleanly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Pulse Width Modulation \(PWM\) generation and Dead-Time insertion for power electronic converters.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   PWM Generation and Shoot-Through Protection:) Tj
T*
(   1. Sinusoidal PWM Generation:) Tj
T*
(   - A high-frequency triangular carrier wave \(frequency fc ~ 2 to 20 kHz\) is compared with a) Tj
T*
(   sinusoidal reference modulating wave \(frequency fm = 50 Hz\).) Tj
T*
(   - Comparator outputs a train of variable-width rectangular pulses whose duty ratio varies) Tj
T*
(   sinusoidally.) Tj
T*
(   - Modulation Index: ma = V_mod / V_carrier.) Tj
T*
(   2. Half-Bridge Inverter Leg Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   - Each phase leg consists of an upper high-side switch \(Q1\) and lower low-side switch \(Q2\)) Tj
T*
(   across DC bus Vdc.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Shoot-Through Catastrophe:) Tj
T*
(   - Power semiconductors \(IGBTs, MOSFETs\) take finite time to turn OFF \(turn-off storage and fall) Tj
T*
(   time\).) Tj
T*
(   - If the upper switch is commanded ON at the exact instant the lower switch is commanded OFF,) Tj
T*
(   both switches conduct simultaneously for a few hundred nanoseconds, short-circuiting the DC bus) Tj
T*
(   \('Shoot-Through'\).) Tj
T*
(   - The resulting thousand-ampere current spike instantly vaporizes both switches.) Tj
T*
(   4. Dead-Time Insertion:) Tj
T*
(   - Microcontroller PWM timer units \(e.g., ARM Cortex-M Advanced Timers\) insert a programmable) Tj
T*
(   hardware delay \('Dead-Time', typically 1 to 3 microseconds\) where BOTH switches are held firmly) Tj
T*
(   OFF before the complementary switch is allowed to turn ON.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
